hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  one key theme that runs throughout  Rainer Maria Rilke is letters to a young  poet is the relationship between art on  the one hand and criticism of art on the  other hand now if you're thinking  criticism is always derogatory or  negation allure something like that he  doesn't actually mean that what he's  talking about is something that we're  you know much more familiar with in  terms of other things you know movies  albums that come out other music  criticism can mean saying something is  good saying it's bad saying why that's  the case it involves judgment  so it's judgements upon art rather than  being art itself and why do we indeed  have criticism at all  well you know it's in part because  that's the way we publicize and call  attention to and have conversations  about art otherwise art might linger by  itself over in the corner and criticism  encompasses a lot later on he's going to  talk about journalism about criticism  per se about literature in which  sometimes criticism will be embedded we  might talk about philosophy of art and  beauty we could talk about all sorts of  things  Rilke himself is rather  down on criticism he thinks that 