ng  anyone whether they are good or not nor  will you try to interest magazines in  these works why you will see them as  your dear natural possession a piece of  your life a voice from it and here's  where he has a very wonderful phrase a  work of art is good if it is arisen out  of necessity that is the only way one  can judge it Rilke says if it is arisen  out of necessity the necessity of the  artist him or herself needing to create  and not just to create or to express  themselves but to create this to do this  that is what makes a piece of art truly  valuable  according to Rilke a little bit later he  talks about how criticism is really  unsuited to reach art where it is he  says for this reason read as little as  possible of literary criticism why  because literary criticism is one of two  things  it's either partisan opinions he says  which have become petrified and  meaningless hardened and empty of life  so they don't have the liveliness of the  work of art which you can have a direct  relationship to either they become mad  or they are merely clever word games and  he says in which case one wins and  tomorrow the opposite view wins that's  not useful either so we don't want some  ossified classification systems and we  don't want clever word games jockeying  for a position in the art world or  anything along those lines what do we  want instead what can we use in order to  grasp art he says works of art are of an  infinite solitude I Nizam kite in German  this is a central concept for Rilke in  letters to a young poet no means of  approach is so useless as criticism  because they don't get into the work of  art what then allows us to get into a  work of art he said  love only love can touch and hold them  and be fair to them always trust  yourself and your own feeling as opposed  to argumentation discussions or  introductions of that sort if it turns  out that you're wrong you go and you see  an artwork and you respond to it and you  have your feeling res