ace they  take their cues from other people within  the art world as some you know recent  estheticians have have dubbed it and  they miss out on a lot of really good  art as a result who fits into this group  here's where Elka says some really funny  things he says all of journalism now  you're saying about the journalism of  his time all of journalism does this  journalism oriented towards art and  almost all criticism so he's not saying  that all criticism is necessarily doing  it just almost all of it and he goes on  to say 3/4 of what is called and wants  to be called literature a lot of the  literature is a little bit too art  focused it's not doing its own creative  thing it's merely invoking tropes and  references to and omage ah's and  pastiche --is and all of this sort of  stuff not really providing a genuine  grounded artistic locus of its own so he  goes on and he says avoid these half  artistic professions you know says art  is just a way of living however one  lives one can without knowing prepare  for it  in everything real one is closer to it  more its neighbor than in these unreal  half artistic professions so he's  telling this this this man who's now  doing his military duty service you can  in fact be closer to art than the art  critics then the journalists right  then the literature's who are invoking  it the last thing I want to point out is  there is one section in here in letter 9  where Rilke talks about criticism in a  positive sense but it would be something  that one would do for oneself he talks  about mr. coppices doubt it's vile and  German right which is a you know being  caught between two different things he  says your doubt be can become a good  quality if you train it it must become  knowing it must become criticism he says  ask it whenever it wants to spoil  something for you why something is ugly  demand proofs from a tested you'll find  it perhaps bewildered and embarrassed  perhaps also protesting but don't give  in insist on 